The flagship experiment concluded at 01:30, and the project has its namesake result:

What the aggregates first suggested: adaptation looked like a failure — choice under the +30° prism never cleanly recovered (bouncing 49–67% across 1M steps), and prism-off performance "degraded" to 54.7% from the 76.4% baseline.

What the conditionals revealed: that degradation is lawful. With the prism removed, when the blue decoy happens to stand ~30° on the minus side of red — precisely where a reach recalibrated against a +30° displacement would aim — AB takes it 92.1% of the time. On the opposite side: 8.3%. The reach now errs in the direction opposite the trained displacement. That is the textbook negative aftereffect, the same phenomenon as a human reaching wrong for a few minutes after taking off prism goggles.

And it survives both rival explanations:
- "It was always like that" — no: pre-adaptation, the same conditional is symmetric (26% vs 21%). Training created it.
