What this project is about

It's a computational test of a theory of perception — specifically the idea its source theorist called interpenetration: that the senses don't develop as separate modules that later get "fused," but develop sequentially (touch and proprioception first, vision later), with each new sense woven into the existing sensory fabric rather than bolted on beside it. Seeing an apple and touching an apple end up activating the same internal representation because vision was built on top of the tactile understanding, not associated with it.

To test this, you built an embodied reinforcement-learning agent — the "Alien Baby" (AB) — that learns to reach and touch a target using proprioception (joint state + a touch bit) and vision (a camera). The body evolved over the project: a 3-joint tabletop arm (v1–v5) → a legless platform creature (v6–v8) → the MIMo infant crawler (the current body, belly-crawling on a platform).

The project is named for its flagship goal: reproducing the classic prism-adaptation aftereffect from human psychology — put on goggles that shift vision sideways, you misreach, you adapt; take the goggles off and you misreach the opposite way (the "negative aftereffect"). That aftereffect is the signature of genuine visuomotor recalibration, and reproducing it in a learned pixels-to-action system was the north star.

What the findings are

The arc is a long fight to get vision to actually matter, followed by a payoff:

The methodology fights (v1–v8, MICOA runs). Early on, "interpenetration" was mis-measured — v1's CKA=0.033 was first read as strong interpenetration, then correctly reinterpreted as vision destroying the proprio representation. The fix was a recipe: freeze the proprio trunk + add a consistency loss pulling vision onto proprio's manifold (v5: CKA=0.998, real "incorporation"). But across dozens of runs vision kept being integrated but inert — the camera latent was nearly identical whether pixels were present or zeroed. A blind proprio policy hit 60% touch while every vision follow-on sat at ~5%: vision was actively destroying a working policy.

The representation breakthrough (Stage A/B/C, MIMo crawler). The root cause turned out to be a representation failure that had persisted "for years": bearing-from-pixels decoded at R²≈0.01–0.08. Stage A fixed it by distilling bearing from pixels into the encoder — R²=0.84. Then Stage C (grow vision by reward alone) stayed inert, while Stage B (distil-then-RL) worked. Verdict: reward alone can't grow visual steering; you must build the representation first, then let RL use it.

Making vision behaviorally necessary (head-search). On a narrow ±22° cone, forward-crawl reached everything, so vision was net-zero. Widening to a ±68° cone with a head-swivel action finally made vision load-bearing — first genuine vision win, sharpened by a narrow→wide curriculum (+22.5-point ablation gap). Replicated across three seeds (sign robust, magnitude 7.5–22.5).

But a subtlety: the prism precheck showed this vision was arousal/presence, not direction — displacing the visual field didn't move the reach. Vision was a "something's there, start searching" cue, not a bearing signal.

The decoy task cracked direction. A two-ball task (red target + blue decoy, wrong-touch penalized, exchangeable placement so blind = 50% by construction) produced the first above-chance visual discrimination — vision choosing which object to reach. Seed-robust (63–78%), and it strengthened with more training.

The breakthroughs

Four stand out, in ascending order of significance:

1. Distil-then-RL as the recipe that finally made vision load-bearing — solving a representation failure that had blocked the project for its entire prior history.
2. First above-chance visual discrimination (decoy task) — pixel color measurably steering target selection with no geometric escape for the blind baseline.
3. Prism displacement: "AB follows the ghost." Rotating the visual field made choice-vs-true fall below chance at 60–90° in both strong seeds — arousal-gating can only degrade toward 50%, never below, so systematic mis-selection proves the displaced picture is steering the reach. Vision is now genuinely direction-carrying.
4. The flagship — negative aftereffect confirmed (2026-07-08). Adapt under a +30° prism, remove it, and the reach now lawfully aims ~30° the opposite way: P(take blue | blue on the −30° side of red) = 92.1% vs 8.3% on the other side, red-anchored, absent in the symmetric pre-adaptation control. Both "relearning" and "arousal" accounts were refuted; recalibration confirmed. This is the classic prism-adaptation phenomenon reproduced end-to-end in a learned sensorimotor system built from pixels, proprioception, and touch — the experiment the project is named for.

Two recent add-ons: the visual color discrimination is shape-invariant (trained only on spheres, discriminates red-from-blue at full strength on boxes/capsules zero-shot — object-agnosticism by learning, since vision could see shape and chose not to bind to it); and the learned rule is specifically "approach red" (positive attraction to the red channel), not "avoid blue" — nailed down by a recolor test where AB chases a red decoy even when red is the wrong answer.

Suggested next steps

The honest caveats in the log point the way. In rough priority:

1. Free diagnostic first (no compute). The theory monitor's own recommendation: re-bin the aftereffect episodes already on disk by bearing-distance from the trained −30° direction, and check whether the wrong-ball rate changes smoothly (a structured spatial remap) or is a flat step on either side of a line (a single global "subtract 30°" bias). This directly settles the one thing the aftereffect result can't yet distinguish — map vs. uniform bias — with data you already have. Do this before spending any more GPU time.
2. Seed-replicate the aftereffect. The flagship was demonstrated on ext_s0 (and displacement on two seeds), but the full adaptation→aftereffect chain is single-seed. Given how much between-seed variance the head-search phase showed, replicating the negative aftereffect on s2 (the clean, non-lateralized 77% discriminator) is the highest-value confirmation before publishing the headline.
