Reframing the goal: from "test a theory" to "demonstrate a rival grounding architecture"

Here's the thing that jumps out from your own FINDINGS log. The dominant way AI grounds language today - vision-language models like GPT-4V or LLaVA - bolts a statically pretrained, web-contrastive vision encoder (CLIP-style) onto a finished language model. In AB's own vocabulary, that is the "all-at-once fusion" architecture - and you have eight months of evidence that this architecture produces brittle, non-interpenetrated representations that fail catastrophically under perturbation, versus the developmentally-staged, sensorimotor-grounded representations that recalibrate, generalize object-agnostically, and degrade gracefully.

So the contribution to AI isn't "we proved a perception theory." It's: current AI grounds language in exactly the way AB proved is the weak way, and AB embodies the alternative. The research program becomes - build a language-capable system whose grounding is developmental and sensorimotor rather than contrastive and static, and test whether it inherits the robustness and genuine directional grounding AB has and CLIP-style grounding demonstrably lacks. That's a claim you can actually defend, because half of it is already in your results.

The honest gap you have to cross: what "underpins language" really means

Your assumption - "our perceptions are the underpinnings for the language we use" - is a real and respectable position (it's the core of embodied/grounded cognition: Lakoff & Johnson, Barsalou, and the Gibsonian lineage your source theory clearly sits in). But there's a scope problem worth naming: AB grounds a handful of invariants - bearing, distance, approach-vs-away, color-as-affordance, and now a recalibratable visionâaction map. Human perception grounds thousands. AB is never going to ground "democracy" or "Tuesday."

The bridge that makes this tractable - and it's a beautiful one - is the image-schema / metaphor-extension claim from that same literature: abstract language is metaphorically extended from a small set of bodily-spatial schemas (source-path-goal, near-far, containment, balance, toward-away). "Grasp an idea," "a close relationship," "I see what you mean," "where is this heading" - the abstract vocabulary is scaffolded on the sensorimotor core. That means you don't need AB to ground the whole lexicon. You need it to ground the sensorimotor primitives that everything else is claimed to be built from. AB already has several of those primitives as measured, load-bearing quantities (mean_toward, bearing, the recalibration map). That's the actual, narrow, defensible bridge - and it's the part current LLMs get purely from text statistics with no grounding at all.

I'd also gently flag the "perceives like humans" step. Even with locomotion, AB perceives like a legless crawler with 32x32 stereo vision reaching for balls. What transfers to the human case is not the content of its percepts but the mechanism - interpenetration, response-conditioning, developmental order. Keep the claim at the mechanism level and it stays honest.

Why "weights as a governor" doesn't type-check - and the steelman

Literally: AB's weights are a policy network mapping (joint angles, touch bit, stereo pixels) â (torques, head yaw). An LLM's weights are a transformer over token embeddings. They live in incompatible spaces with no shared coordinate system - there's no defined operation by which one "governs" the other. Bolting AB's weight matrices onto the LLM has no meaning.

The steelman - the thing that does transfer - is not the weight tensors but the grounded representational structure: the invariants AB learned and the consistency constraints that shaped them. The real question is how you anchor an LLM's concepts to those grounded structures. Four ways to do it, cheapest to most radical:

(a) Concept-anchoring probe - the cheap first experiment, almost no build. Take AB's encoder latent for a set of states, take an LLM's hidden activations for the matching words ("toward," "away," "left," "near," "reach"), and measure whether a linear map aligns them (CCA / linear probe). If the LLM's internal direction for "toward" maps onto AB's mean_toward invariant, you have direct evidence for your underpinning hypothesis - that the word and the sensorimotor invariant share structure - before building anything. If they don't align, that's the most interesting result of all: it says text-trained "toward" is ungrounded, which is your whole thesis.

(b) Grounded soft-prompt bridge - the direct A/B test of your thesis. This is mechanically the same trick VLMs use (learn a small adapter that projects a perceptual latent into the LLM's embedding space as a prefix/soft-prompt), with one decisive difference: the encoder is AB's developmentally-grounded sensorimotor encoder, not CLIP. Then run the head-to-head that no one has run: AB-grounded LLM vs. CLIP-grounded LLM on spatial/affordance/embodied-reasoning tasks. If the sensorimotor grounding wins on exactly the robustness and directional-consistency dimensions AB is strong on, you've shown developmental grounding beats contrastive grounding - a genuinely publishable, fundable claim.

(c) Governor-as-consistency-critic - the faithful realization of your intuition. Your "governor on the engine" maps cleanly onto something real if you make it a critic, not a filter. When the LLM produces a claim with sensorimotor content, AB's grounded model scores whether it's consistent with how an embodied perception-action loop actually behaves - like an RLHF reward model, but the reward is embodied-perceptual plausibility instead of human preference. And note: this is just AB's own consistency loss scaled up. AB already enforces "vision must agree with proprio." The governor enforces "language must agree with grounded perception." Same primitive, one level up. That parallel is not a coincidence - it's the theory doing exactly what it should.

(d) Foundation, not governor - the radical, most theory-faithful reading. Here's the fork your own words point at. "Governor" is a post-hoc constraint on a finished LLM. But your sentence - "perceptions are the underpinnings for language" - implies the opposite architecture: the grounded system is the base, and language is interpenetrated onto it the way vision was interpenetrated onto proprio. That's the theory-faithful version: don't connect a finished LLM to AB; grow language on AB's grounded substrate. And you already discovered the recipe for it - distil-then-RL: reward (or next-token prediction) alone can't grow grounding; you build the representation first, then let the higher process use it. That is a direct, pointed critique of how LLMs are trained today (language modeling alone, no grounded substrate) and it comes straight out of your Stage A/B/C result.

My recommendation

Don't start with the big build. The order that respects both the theory and your compute budget:

1. Run the concept-anchoring probe (a) first. It's near-zero cost, uses checkpoints already on disk plus any open LLM, and it either gives you first evidence for the underpinning hypothesis or falsifies it - before you commit to architecture. This is the embodied-cognition equivalent of the free aftereffect diagnostic I flagged last turn.
2. If it aligns, build the grounded soft-prompt bridge (b) and run the AB-grounded-vs-CLIP-grounded head-to-head. That's the experiment that turns this from philosophy into a result.
3. Hold (c)/(d) as the program's north star - the governor-as-consistency-critic and eventually language-interpenetrated-onto-grounding - but let (a) and (b) tell you whether the bridge is real before you invest in growing it.

The single most important decision you're actually making here is governor vs. foundation (post-hoc constraint on a finished LLM, or grounded substrate that language grows on top of). Your theory implies foundation; the field's tools make governor far easier to prototype. I'd prototype the governor to get signal fast, while being clear in the writeup that the theory-faithful endpoint is foundation.

