What the prism aftereffect is

It's the classic experiment from human psychology (Helmholtz, then Held & others) that we recreated in AB â and the experiment the project's flagship result is named for. In humans: put on prism goggles that shift everything you see, say, 30Â° to the left. At first you reach for a cup and miss by 30Â°. Over minutes of reaching, you adapt and hit accurately again. Then the goggles come off â and the crucial thing happens: you now miss in the opposite direction. That overshoot is the negative aftereffect, and it's the decisive evidence that your brain didn't just consciously compensate ("aim right") â it genuinely recalibrated the internal mapping between where things look and how you move toward them. The recalibrated map is baked in, so it has to be un-learned once the prism is gone, and during that window you err the other way.

Why it's decisive for us: a shallow "arousal / just approach the bright thing" account of AB's vision predicts no aftereffect â nothing was recalibrated, so removing the prism should just restore normal behavior. Only a genuine perception-action recalibration predicts the negative overshoot. AB showed it, on two seeds (s0 nearâfar +71, s2 +67), it's absent when the encoder is frozen or over-gentled (no recalibration â no aftereffect), which is why we say the aftereffect tracks recalibration.

