Governor vs. fine-tune vs. build-new â one axis, three points

These aren't three unrelated options; they're three points on a single axis of how deeply the grounded signal is allowed to change the language model, trading ceiling against cost and against forgetting:

1. Governor (frozen LLM, external critic â architecture c). AB's model sits outside a finished LLM and scores/vetoes/reweights its outputs. Cheap, reversible, interpretable, fast signal. But its ceiling is hard-bounded: a governor can only reweight what the LLM already latently represents â it cannot install a representation the model never had. And here's the rub the probe results expose: the meaning-cloud test showed the LLM's spatial vocabulary is phrasing-contingent and not stably grounded. If the geometry you want isn't reliably in there, a governor has little to grab onto. So the very evidence we have argues a pure governor will hit a low ceiling on exactly the spatial-meaning case we care about.

2. Fine-tune an existing model (train a small adapter, or update LLM weights with grounded signal â architecture b, and its heavier RL cousin). This is the pragmatic middle and, I'd argue, the actual sweet spot. The soft-prompt bridge (b) freezes the LLM and trains only a small projector from AB's encoder into the LLM's embedding space â cheap, and it can install new structure at the input without catastrophic forgetting. Heavier: use AB's model as a reward signal to fine-tune the LLM weights (RLHF-style, but reward = embodied-perceptual plausibility). That can genuinely install geometry the model lacked, at the cost of expense and forgetting risk. This is where "governor" and "fine-tune" actually blur â the soft-prompt bridge is a minimal fine-tune.

3. Build a new model / our own embeddings (foundation â architecture d, + your atomization/physics-of-meaning ideas). Grow language on the grounded substrate rather than bolt it on. Highest ceiling â it changes what representations exist at all â but Â§8's conditional bites hard: foundation only wins to the extent the grounded substrate is broad. Grow language on toward/away/red and you get a stunted speaker. The meaning-cloud result is the strongest current argument for this path (the phrasing-invariant meaning geometry lives in grounding, not in text embeddings, so you may have to build it), and your two captured ideas â atomization of meaning, physics of meaning â are the how. But it's the hardest build and the substrate isn't broad yet.

My read on sequencing given the evidence: the meaning-cloud result already tells us a pure governor (1) is probably ceiling-limited for spatial meaning, so I wouldn't over-invest there â use it only as the cheap de-risk to confirm grounded structure carries any actionable signal. The highest-value next move is the adapter bridge (2): it's the direct A/B test of the whole thesis, it's cheap, and it's the same first step whether you ultimately aim at fine-tune or foundation. Build-new (3) is the theory-faithful north star but is gated on both a positive bridge result and a broader substrate â which is the direct incentive to keep enriching AB's perception. In short: governor to sniff, adapter to test, foundation to aim at â and the evidence so far tilts the eventual endpoint toward build-new, because that's where the phrasing-invariant meaning actually lives.

One caveat worth flagging: "our own embeddings" (foundation) and "fine-tune an existing model" can also be composed â Â§8 already names the likely real winner as a hybrid: text-pretrained breadth with a grounded core grown/fine-tuned on the substrate. It's not strictly either/or.

